# Hostile audit — Section 127 face fidelity and global defect injection

Date: 2026-09-13

Reviewed artifact: `research/target_c_section127_face_fidelity_global_defect_injection.md`

Disposition: PASS at proof-only scope. The theorem state and the C-II status are
unchanged.

## 1. Cell-interior vertex statement

Section 82 places every vertex of an unloaded component `I_r` in the interior of its
attachment polygon `P_r`. Section 83 makes distinct cell interiors disjoint. Therefore
an interior point of `P_r` cannot be a vertex of another unloaded component.

If a loaded hull vertex `b` is not in `A_r`, then `A_r` is contained in
`B\{b}`. Since `b` is an extreme point of `conv(B)`, it is not in
`conv(B\{b})`, and hence not in `P_r`. This proves

`V(G) cap int(P_r) = I_r`.

Verdict: valid, with contact-isolated full-source points outside `V(G)` as required.

## 2. Local bounded faces are global faces

The interior of a bounded face of the induced graph `H_r` lies in `int(P_r)` and
contains no vertex of `H_r`. The cell-interior statement then excludes every other
positive-core vertex from that interior.

Any positive-core contact edge not in `H_r` cannot meet that open face. If it entered
and left the face, a proper crossing with its Jordan boundary would occur. If both
boundary intersections were vertices, both endpoints would lie in `I_r union A_r`,
so inducedness would put the edge in `H_r`; this also cannot cut the face. Tangential
contact at a boundary vertex does not enter the open face. Thus the open face contains
neither an omitted core vertex nor an omitted core edge, and is a bounded face of the
full positive-contact graph.

The argument does not require contact-isolated source points to be absent: they have
no contact edges and do not alter the positive-contact embedding.

Verdict: valid. The straight-line planarity and inducedness hypotheses are used at the
only possible multiplicity points.

## 3. Exceptional-perimeter injection

Distinct local bounded faces cannot be the same global face because their open
interiors would lie in two disjoint cell interiors. Hence local nonquadrilateral-face
degrees inject into the global face occurrences, giving

`sum_r P_exc,r <= 4f + Q`.

Every nonquadrilateral bounded face has degree at least five, so `f <= Q`. With the
canonical identity

`e_BB = Q + 2k + u_B + c_B`,

all terms nonnegative and `k=d_H+s_Z`, this also gives

`sum_r P_exc,r <= 5(e_BB-k)`.

The latter is not stronger than the Type-II `5(e_BB-2k)` payment from the separate
augmented-triangle argument; no replacement of the sharper Type-II theorem is made.

Verdict: valid, with the scope distinction retained.

## 4. Limiting and closure boundary

Combining the finite injection with Section 122 yields only

`sum_r(L_r-2d_r) >= (4-2sqrt(3)) A_4^0/D - (4f+Q)`.

In Type II this is a lower demand after subtracting a potentially linear defect term.
It does not upper-bound the aggregate outer detour, force a unit equilateral triangle,
or produce a source-preserving strict sub-`D` configuration. Macroscopic inactive
containers and contact-isolated points remain outside the claim.

Verdict: no C-II closure is implied.

## Final audit verdict

Section 127 is a sound proof-only face-fidelity and defect-injection theorem. Its
strongest safe use is bookkeeping for future source-level detour or descent arguments;
it must not be promoted to a global C-II conclusion.
